00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.470
PROFESSOR: All right
welcome back, everyone.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:27.480
I can't wait to hear about
your projects and trips.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.000
So today you guys are
going to do presentations.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.520
Then we're going to go
through a really quick review

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.500
of the design process,
which is appropriate,

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.710
because we have a really quick
design process to go through.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.170
And then you guys
will do probably

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:41.610
no brainstorming--
maybe a little,

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.500
but then doing Pugh
charts to figure out

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.530
what projects make sense for the
class to work on the semester.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.198
And then part of
that will be you're

00:00:48.198 --> 00:00:50.490
going to do micro presentations
at the end of the class

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.340
that you'll prep during
class so that you guys can

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.090
select what projects you're
going to work on tonight.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.480
Are there any questions or
anything to note while you--

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.220
before we get started?

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:03.720
OK.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.170
And so ideally, we'll do
GrupoFenix, first, and then

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:08.970
ASAPROSAR, and then AsoFenix.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:14.160
OK, so do you guys
want to start?

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.670
AUDIENCE: So hi, everyone.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:17.970
For those of you who
don't know, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:18.870
We're GrupoFenix.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.990
We traveled to
Sabana Grande, which

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:24.720
is 20 minutes south
of Ocotal, which

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.320
is one of the bigger cities in
Nicaragua, for spring break.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.890
And we were there for five, six
days, and we had a lot of fun.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:36.150
So the first thing
I'll talk about--

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:37.767
this is our group.

00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:38.850
I'll talk about our group.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:39.808
AUDIENCE: That's Jorge.

00:01:39.808 --> 00:01:41.257
AUDIENCE: And Jorge--

00:01:41.257 --> 00:01:42.840
I guess we should
introduce ourselves.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.700
I'm Jessica.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.050
AUDIENCE: I'm Vivian.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:47.100
AUDIENCE: Liz--

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.065
AUDIENCE: Kurt--

00:01:48.065 --> 00:01:48.690
AUDIENCE: Sid--

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:49.357
AUDIENCE: Juan--

00:01:49.357 --> 00:01:50.190
AUDIENCE: Beautiful.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:51.210
And this is Jorge.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.900
He is the shop manager field--

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.790
onsite field manager guy.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:57.630
He's the go-to guy.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:00.570
Basically, you hear
his name over 300 times

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.178
in an hour being shouted.

00:02:02.178 --> 00:02:02.970
Jorge, get us this.

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.300
Jorge, get us that--

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.270
so really handy guy.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:09.990
Were we able to give
him a D-Lab [INAUDIBLE]??

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:13.167
No-- so next time.

00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:15.000
So we're not going to
talk about this first.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.400
We're going to talk about
the charcoal burn first.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.500
So when we got
there, the itinerary

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:20.970
was to do a lot
of charcoal burns,

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:23.070
because that was one
of our projects--

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:24.420
possible projects to work on.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.640
And there's a lot of
opportunity there,

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.620
because when we got
there on Monday,

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.980
we had our first burn with
them, and they showed us

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.780
the briquettes
that they had been

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.200
able to make from
their Friday burn.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.800
And this is what came out of it.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.800
And as you see it
from-- if you remember

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.100
from our briquette making--
it's really great, especially

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.950
when you compare it to the
blackness of this darker

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.050
charcoal, which is
a successful burn

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:51.723
and a successful briquette
made while we were there.

00:02:51.723 --> 00:02:53.140
So this is what
we made with them.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.557
This is what they had made the
Friday before we got there.

00:02:55.557 --> 00:02:58.140
And there was improvement-- yay.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:01.230
In this picture, you
see all the white smoke,

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:04.050
which is basically the water
vapor from the barrel burning.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.575
And it's because, when
they used the Friday--

00:03:06.575 --> 00:03:07.950
this was used in
the same biomass

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.650
that they used Friday,
which is this corn stock.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.200
And this corn stock
apparently wasn't dry enough.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.670
It seemed like it
was dry enough,

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.860
but only when we
were burning it did

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.980
we see that it wasn't actually
dry enough for the briquettes.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:22.410
At the end of our
week there, we were

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:26.410
able to make over 150
briquettes from the two burns.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:29.310
And so we yielded
150 briquettes.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.710
Before they were only
making six to seven

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:33.930
of these briquettes
for each charcoal burn.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.000
And they were looking
at those frantically,

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.890
because when we got
there, they already

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.560
expressed that they did have
a big interest in charcoal

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.230
making, because
they're starting--

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:45.870
this is a solar center, and
on the other side of the field

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:48.450
is the restaurant that they're
going to start operating.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.400
And one of the fuels
that they want to use

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.270
is charcoal briquettes to
cook in the restaurant.

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:57.840
So they wanted to make this
economically feasible and ready

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.550
to sell for the people
of Sabana Grande,

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.530
possibly to resell it to Ocotal.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:08.970
And so we were able to get
a better yield for them.

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:10.810
Some quick lessons learned--

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:12.660
so a little bit of the history--

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:15.690
Amy came in after Judy.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:17.940
Judy is a random
service volunteer

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.869
that came to the
women of Totogalpa,

00:04:19.869 --> 00:04:22.740
to the Solar Women of
Totogalpa, and taught them

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:24.090
how to do the charcoal burns.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:27.060
But apparently she taught them
completely wrong, because she--

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.310
the timing on covering the--

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:32.107
on sealing the barrel,
like we all know, was off.

00:04:32.107 --> 00:04:33.690
And so because of
that, they were only

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:35.970
yielding six to seven, and
it was burning too much,

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:37.080
so it was getting ashy.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:41.063
She didn't teach
them little checks.

00:04:41.063 --> 00:04:41.730
So what we did--

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:44.430
I'm sorry-- what we did is
that Kurt and I sat down

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:47.775
with two charcoal
captains, we called them.

00:04:47.775 --> 00:04:49.650
Two of the women were
made charcoal captains,

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:52.380
because Kurt and I
briefed them extensively

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:54.840
for like a four-hour
period and instructed them

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:59.010
on what the instructions
meant, the diagrammed ones

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:00.118
that we have in Spanish.

00:05:00.118 --> 00:05:01.660
And so we went over
the translations,

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:04.630
because the Spanish that it
was translated into sometimes

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:06.250
was a little different
in Nicaraguan,

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:08.305
so we did that
translation so that they

00:05:08.305 --> 00:05:09.430
could understand it better.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:12.730
And then we made
them in charge of--

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:15.220
as captains, they
chose to other women,

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:17.950
and that-- those four women
were the charcoal committee.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:20.620
So these two women,
the captains,

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.935
instructed the other two
women on the process.

00:05:22.935 --> 00:05:25.060
So now it's a little bit
more of an internal thing.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:27.610
You also learn better
when you teach, right?

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:29.740
And so by the time that
we got there on Thursday,

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:31.060
we regrouped.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:33.910
And on Thursday we did the
charcoal burn in the morning,

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:36.220
with all four of them
just leading the way.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:39.160
They would say every step of
the process before they did it,

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.260
and it showed that they
really picked up on it.

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:44.170
And by the afternoon, they
did another burn basically

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:46.087
completely by themselves.

00:05:46.087 --> 00:05:47.920
The successful key
points that they really--

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:50.020
that really showed us that they
knew what they were talking

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:52.630
about was when we would ask
them in the middle of it all,

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:55.157
just to check, how do
you know if it's good--

00:05:55.157 --> 00:05:55.990
if it's a good burn?

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:58.180
And they would say, oh,
you got to crack it open,

00:05:58.180 --> 00:05:59.650
and the heart has to be black.

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:00.700
And I would say, OK.

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:03.250
How do if the
biomass is too wet?

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:04.630
They would say,
oh, because you'd

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:06.433
have a lot of white
smoke, and that's bad.

00:06:06.433 --> 00:06:08.350
And I'm like, so what's
the yellow smoke then?

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:10.517
And then they'd say, oh,
those are the contaminants.

00:06:10.517 --> 00:06:11.650
And I'm like, contaminants?

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:13.160
But that's what
the wood has when

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:14.410
you're cooking with it, right?

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:16.447
And they go, no, it's
different, because--

00:06:16.447 --> 00:06:19.030
and then they would be able to
explain all the process and all

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:21.700
the logic, and really
get to the why of things.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:22.260
Kurt--

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:22.885
AUDIENCE: Cool.

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:33.090
For the Thursday burns
that Jess described,

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:35.040
we went out to the
fields and collected

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.410
biomass that had been
sitting there for a month.

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:39.270
So it was way drier.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:45.900
And so this prompted us to think
about a possible project for us

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:46.800
to work on--

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:48.460
at least something
to think about,

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:52.920
which is whether to bring
the biomass into the center

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:56.190
and burn it there or figure
out some way of being

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:58.980
able to do the
burns at the fields

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:01.530
where all this dry biomass is.

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:04.170
And because Sabana Grande's
quite a large area,

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:05.910
we thought maybe that
could be something

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:08.580
that we could think about,
particularly from the cost

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:12.700
perspective, which Liz is going
to talk about in a little bit.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:16.770
But other things that
we're looking at as far

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:21.750
as possible projects are
also this solar dryer

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:23.850
for briquettes,
because we discovered,

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:27.900
in interviewing the women,
that the biggest problem they

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:32.580
had with the process was having
to air dry these briquettes

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:34.410
for three to four days outside.

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:37.200
And they didn't want to
take them outside each day

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.370
and then have to
bring them back in,

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:41.500
and then be worried
always about the rain.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:44.100
So they work on these--

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:47.340
their primary work is
on the solar ovens,

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:49.230
but they also do solar dryers.

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:51.960
So we experimented a little
bit with using these,

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:54.210
and we think that one
project we could work on

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:58.380
is a solar dryer specifically
for charcoal briquettes

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:03.810
so they can start making
these really quickly as--

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:06.360
almost like a
factory so that they

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:09.020
can supply them to
their restaurant

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:11.010
and achieve scale there.

00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:15.690
And so this shows some
more of the process.

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:19.260
This is the new biomass that we
brought in that was way dryer.

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:20.900
This is our second burn.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:30.860
And this was the
briquette making process.

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:33.789
So one other thing
that we thought

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:37.000
could be a potential
project is experimenting

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:41.049
with the like cornstarch
versus yuca versus using banana

00:08:41.049 --> 00:08:45.340
starch, because they have--
they can just by cornstarch

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:47.950
there pretty cheaply, and
it's much easier for them

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:52.660
to do that than have to grind
up this yuca for binder.

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:56.260
So those are basically
the range of projects

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:59.440
that we're thinking about
right now, as far as charcoal.

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:02.150
And if you want to talk a little
bit about the cost study now--

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:03.880
AUDIENCE: So we
also, really quickly,

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:08.140
did cost study so that we
could ensure that teaching them

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:10.840
charcoal was actually useful,
because they basically wanted

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:15.910
to get paid 10 cordoba an hour--
which is like $0.50 an hour--

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:18.730
and work seven-hour days.

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:21.220
And so based on those
numbers and roughly

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:24.580
how many burns we thought
they could do in one day,

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:27.085
if they just did like
a bunch of burns in one

00:09:27.085 --> 00:09:30.205
day and just had a few
going at a time maybe,

00:09:30.205 --> 00:09:32.080
we thought that they
would be able to sell it

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:34.810
for slightly cheaper than you
could buy it in Ocotal, which

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:37.900
was a 20-minute bus ride away,
so that it could actually

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:38.960
be a feasible option.

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:40.960
And they also really
wanted to use this charcoal

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:41.793
in their restaurant.

00:09:41.793 --> 00:09:44.170
So hopefully, if they
really get into this

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:46.810
and they get a lot better
at it, then they'll

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:49.630
be able to actually
use charcoal,

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:55.142
and it'll be economically viable
as well as a clean alternative.

00:09:55.142 --> 00:09:57.100
I'm also going to talk
about solar ovens, which

00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:59.570
I think-- here we go.

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:01.630
This is their solar
oven that they

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:05.320
have all over the solar center.

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:07.780
It's sort of the
symbol of solar woman

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:10.563
that we were working with,
because it's something

00:10:10.563 --> 00:10:11.230
that they build.

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:13.238
They paint them
this bright color.

00:10:13.238 --> 00:10:15.280
They all work a lot of
hours in the solar center,

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:17.690
and then when they build
up a certain number

00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:19.940
of hundreds of hours, they
can buy one for themselves.

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:21.550
So some of the women
have them at home.

00:10:21.550 --> 00:10:22.810
And they can use
them to make things

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:25.268
like cookies, which you see
here, which they can't actually

00:10:25.268 --> 00:10:27.060
make in--

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:27.940
on a wood fire.

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:29.818
So a lot of the women
really enjoy them.

00:10:29.818 --> 00:10:32.110
And while they're not really
a very economically viable

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:34.880
option, they're a way of
bringing the women together,

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:38.145
which we thought was
really interesting.

00:10:38.145 --> 00:10:39.520
There was a Cornell
group they're

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:42.580
working on these solar ovens to
try to improve their insulation

00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:45.170
and other smaller parts
while we were there,

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:46.450
so we worked a lot with them.

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:49.060
And they actually built a
new oven with fiberglass

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:50.710
insulation while we
were there, so we

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:52.840
got to see how
they were built. We

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:54.880
feel as if there's
not too much more

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:57.100
that we can add to that
project, as opposed

00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:59.240
to the many other
projects that are there,

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:02.590
so really the only projects
that we think we should possibly

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:04.450
pursue with regards
to the solar cookers

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:06.160
was the solar dryer
for the charcoal.

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:11.660
And then that was
the final charcoal

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:14.780
that was supposed to be before.

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:17.120
AUDIENCE: So
[INAUDIBLE] so right

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:23.720
now they make these
panels by hand.

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:27.320
The way it works is that
the group GrupoFenix gets

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:30.857
all of the extra small
panels from the US,

00:11:30.857 --> 00:11:33.440
and they buy boxes of the panels
that don't pass the test here

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:34.310
in the US.

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:40.770
And for about 500 of the
small panels, it's about $500.

00:11:40.770 --> 00:11:42.830
So it's about $1 a cell--

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:45.277
sorry-- a cell--
so about $1 a cell.

00:11:45.277 --> 00:11:47.610
The problem is that, out of
the 500 cells that they get,

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:51.380
there's about 100 to 200
broken, depending on the box.

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:55.170
So it's more than that.

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:57.020
And so this is a really
intensive project--

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:58.770
process of making the
cells-- the panels.

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:00.520
I'm just going to go
over it really quick.

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:03.750
Oh.

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:04.710
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:08.370
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] the
setup, like set everything up.

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:10.410
AUDIENCE: You want to
just talk on this slide,

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:11.535
or was there another slide?

00:12:11.535 --> 00:12:13.050
AUDIENCE: Yeah,
it's the next one.

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:14.040
OK.

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:16.140
Yeah, so first they
connect them all

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:19.170
and they call them
chains, solar cell chains.

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:22.020
They solder everything by hand,
and they test every single cell

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:24.480
by hand and every chain by hand.

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:31.530
Then they move these changes
independently to this paper.

00:12:31.530 --> 00:12:34.890
And then, after they place
each chain independently,

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:38.977
they then add liquid silicon
all over the panels by hand.

00:12:38.977 --> 00:12:41.310
And they have to make sure
that it's evenly spread, one,

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:42.900
and there are no air bubbles.

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:44.820
And then they get
the glass, and they

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:46.290
have to put it evenly on top.

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:52.620
And then, by hand they try to
squish out all the air bubbles

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:54.060
that are still in there.

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:56.980
Then they add foam
and add batteries.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:12:59.130
And this is kind of
a annoying process,

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:01.980
because they have to put
each battery at the same--

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:03.754
two batteries at
the same time so it

00:13:03.754 --> 00:13:05.820
will have even pressure
across the cells.

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:08.550
And they leave it
there for 24 hours so

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:11.010
that the last air
bubbles can escape,

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:14.310
and so that it can be
completely flat and even.

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:16.868
So there's obviously
a lot of improvement.

00:13:16.868 --> 00:13:19.410
It takes about 24 hours to make
one of the large solar cell--

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:20.310
solar panels.

00:13:20.310 --> 00:13:22.470
And they produce
about 220 watts.

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:26.010
So some of the [INAUDIBLE]
we were thinking about were--

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:29.190
he mentioned, instead of
having to place the batteries

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:32.520
to heavier pressure,
Maybe just an even way

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:36.690
that you can just pull down,
and it holds on the pressure.

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:42.330
We were also thinking about
having some type of vacuum pump

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:44.580
or vacuum chamber
that just removes

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:49.440
all the air bubbles so you
don't have to do it by hand.

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:52.200
Oh, there's also another thing.

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:54.870
They're thinking about using
ethyl vinyl acetate, instead of

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:59.920
the liquid silicon, to cover
and insulate the solar cells.

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:04.583
The problem with the EVA is
that it has to be heated heated,

00:14:04.583 --> 00:14:06.750
and then it has-- and then
there's also air bubbles,

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:07.860
even if it does heat up.

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:10.710
So another possible
project is a way

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:14.910
to heat it maybe using the solar
ovens, which they say they did,

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:17.020
or maybe just--
maybe another way.

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:18.645
That could be another
possible project.

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:24.260
OK.

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:29.115
So those are the
large solar panels,

00:14:29.115 --> 00:14:30.740
which they use for
their homes and they

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:32.990
using the power the restaurant,
and they're also using

00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:35.060
to power the solar center.

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:38.030
Then they're trying to make
the small solar cell phone

00:14:38.030 --> 00:14:39.803
chargers, which
you saw last time.

00:14:39.803 --> 00:14:41.720
And they're trying to
basically model to this.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:45.877
This is worth $10 in Ocotal,
and it's made in China.

00:14:45.877 --> 00:14:47.960
The great thing about it
is that it actually comes

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:49.043
with a cell phone battery.

00:14:49.043 --> 00:14:51.013
It fits most Nokia cell phones.

00:14:51.013 --> 00:14:52.430
And what you see
on the right side

00:14:52.430 --> 00:14:54.947
is a charger as well as a lamp.

00:14:54.947 --> 00:14:56.780
So not only can you
charge your cell phone--

00:14:56.780 --> 00:14:59.360
you can use-- also use the
battery to power up a light.

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:00.950
So that would be
the ideal project.

00:15:03.693 --> 00:15:05.360
So yeah, that would
be an ideal project.

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:09.920
Currently, what they're working
on is just the solar cells.

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:11.743
And because they need
a smaller voltage,

00:15:11.743 --> 00:15:13.910
and they don't really have
much access to capacitors

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:16.010
or resistors, they cut--

00:15:16.010 --> 00:15:17.060
go back to-- yeah--

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:21.860
they cut every cell by hand
using this-- using a dremel.

00:15:21.860 --> 00:15:24.328
So that's another cause, and
another way to cut the cells,

00:15:24.328 --> 00:15:26.870
instead of having to do it by
hand, would be another project.

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:30.830
What I have here is a little
old battery cell phone charger.

00:15:30.830 --> 00:15:33.350
So you basically put
your battery here,

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:35.090
and then you move
around, depending

00:15:35.090 --> 00:15:38.420
on where your battery is, and
you can charge your cell phone.

00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:41.280
You can pass it around
if you want to see it.

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:44.010
And so we were thinking
of installing that

00:15:44.010 --> 00:15:46.470
to the solar panel so you could
charge any type of battery,

00:15:46.470 --> 00:15:51.030
not just Nokia, ideally.

00:15:51.030 --> 00:15:52.210
What else?

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:54.962
AUDIENCE: A main issue with that
is also the cost, because we

00:15:54.962 --> 00:15:56.670
were having a lot of
trouble figuring out

00:15:56.670 --> 00:15:58.200
how you would actually--

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:00.750
using their current system,
their prices would be a lot

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:02.310
higher than the China-made one--

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:02.460
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:03.543
AUDIENCE: --which is hard.

00:16:03.543 --> 00:16:05.220
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:06.720
Yeah, right now the
costs are really

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:11.250
the biggest issue, the fact that
each solar cell is about $1.

00:16:11.250 --> 00:16:14.190
Then the silicon-- they use
about 600 grams of the liquid

00:16:14.190 --> 00:16:17.090
silicon right now, and that's--

00:16:17.090 --> 00:16:18.070
I forget the number.

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:22.983
It's all in my head, but I think
it's about $100 for 5 gallons.

00:16:22.983 --> 00:16:24.150
And just everything adds up.

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:26.108
The labor, I think, is
the most intensive part.

00:16:26.108 --> 00:16:30.870
They really use about 12 actual
hours, 24 hours with the drying

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:32.790
included.

00:16:32.790 --> 00:16:35.710
The glass is
relatively expensive,

00:16:35.710 --> 00:16:38.130
and then there's the
aluminum on the side.

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:39.480
There's also the solar--

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:42.248
the tape, that they
have to do it by hand--

00:16:42.248 --> 00:16:43.290
which is not really tape.

00:16:43.290 --> 00:16:45.810
It's the little aluminum strips.

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:48.000
So that could even
be another project.

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:50.460
Maybe have a aluminum tape.

00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:53.820
That way, you can use tape,
instead of having to solder on.

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:57.065
That could be another
great project.

00:16:57.065 --> 00:16:58.440
So we're going to
keep talk-- oh,

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:00.690
and [? Mario. ?] There are
two people that really make

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:03.550
the solar cells right now, which
is the guy in the top left,

00:17:03.550 --> 00:17:06.119
which is [? Mario. ?] And then
there's another [? Mario, ?]

00:17:06.119 --> 00:17:08.369
which is like little [? Mario ?]
and big [? Mario-- ?]

00:17:08.369 --> 00:17:13.440
who's the only male member of
the cooperative of the Solar

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:16.156
Women of Totogalpa.

00:17:16.156 --> 00:17:16.932
Yep.

00:17:16.932 --> 00:17:17.599
That's about it.

00:17:25.415 --> 00:17:26.040
AUDIENCE: Cool.

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:31.680
So once we spent a
couple of days there,

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:34.110
we realized that a
lot of the center

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:38.520
is focused specifically
towards 20 women who

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:40.480
are part of the cooperative.

00:17:40.480 --> 00:17:45.180
And so again, wondering, if
we did design a project based

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:46.950
on what we learned
at the center,

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:49.680
would it only benefit
those 20 people,

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:53.710
or would it help the
surrounding area in general?

00:17:53.710 --> 00:17:56.730
And so this is
actually the only slide

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:59.520
that we have for that
part, but basically,

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:02.340
what we decided to do
is me and [? Kwan ?]

00:18:02.340 --> 00:18:05.940
walked a couple of kilometers
down into another district

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:08.220
called Santa Domingo.

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:09.870
And this is the
road we walked on.

00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:14.700
And so Santa Domingo does
not have great electricity,

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:18.630
so they're basically cut
off from any conventional

00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:20.430
electrical appliances.

00:18:20.430 --> 00:18:22.387
And so we wanted to
go there and ask them

00:18:22.387 --> 00:18:23.970
what their involvement
with the center

00:18:23.970 --> 00:18:29.100
was, if they had considered
other options for solar,

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:31.540
or wind, or whatever.

00:18:31.540 --> 00:18:34.420
And so we asked them
a bunch of questions.

00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:36.180
So the first thing
we asked them was

00:18:36.180 --> 00:18:37.410
if they'd heard of charcoal.

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:42.540
And we interviewed 10 families,
and none of them used charcoal.

00:18:42.540 --> 00:18:44.820
Three of the
families hadn't even

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:47.220
heard of carbon,
which is what-- yeah.

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:50.400
So they didn't know what it was.

00:18:50.400 --> 00:18:54.000
We then went on to ask them
what they did use to cook,

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:56.460
and they all said firewood.

00:18:56.460 --> 00:18:58.890
We asked them where they
get the firewood from.

00:18:58.890 --> 00:19:01.020
All of them get the firewood
from their surrounding

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:02.520
communities, but
there was actually

00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:06.000
one woman who walked
3 kilometers every day

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:08.250
just to get firewood,
which we thought

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:13.290
was kind of unfortunate.

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:17.280
We also asked them
after that if they

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:20.880
do use some form of lighting
at night, and if so, what?

00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:23.160
All the families we
interviewed did have

00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:25.270
some kind of lamps at night.

00:19:25.270 --> 00:19:27.930
Mostly they were diesel lamps.

00:19:27.930 --> 00:19:29.680
We weren't sure exactly
how they worked,

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:37.740
but they all either used diesel
or ocote, which is pinewood,

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:42.000
or electric lamps,
which run on batteries.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.310
So once they said that
they do use batteries,

00:19:44.310 --> 00:19:46.853
we asked them what they
did use batteries for.

00:19:46.853 --> 00:19:49.020
And it turned out that they
basically used batteries

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:50.610
for three things--

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:55.740
lighting, radios, and if
they're big 9-volt batteries,

00:19:55.740 --> 00:19:59.790
then you could power
a cell phone charger.

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:01.300
So once we asked
them about that,

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:04.200
we also asked them about how
many cell phones they have,

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:06.390
or if they did have
any, per family.

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:10.380
And four out of nine of the
families we interviewed had

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:12.420
cell phones, but
the rest didn't.

00:20:12.420 --> 00:20:14.472
And for the families that
did have cell phones,

00:20:14.472 --> 00:20:15.930
we asked them how
they charge them,

00:20:15.930 --> 00:20:18.042
because they weren't
near the grid.

00:20:18.042 --> 00:20:19.500
So apparently, what
they do is they

00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:24.030
walk to a house
which is in the--

00:20:24.030 --> 00:20:28.350
which is in Sabana Grande
to charge their cell phones,

00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:38.182
and are charged 5 to 10 cordoba
for each cycle of recharging.

00:20:38.182 --> 00:20:41.550
Apart from that, we also
asked them if they had cycles.

00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:45.120
This was just out of curiosity,
because we were wondering

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:48.630
if pedal power was any--

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:49.500
if it was viable.

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:52.275
And every family did
have at least one cycle.

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:57.570
Oh, yeah, so a
cell phone battery

00:20:57.570 --> 00:21:00.450
charges for 5 to 10 cordoba,
and a 12-volt battery

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:02.542
charges for 30 cordoba.

00:21:06.080 --> 00:21:07.080
That was pretty much it.

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:09.270
We just wanted to
get some perspective

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:12.807
on off-grid communities.

00:21:12.807 --> 00:21:14.640
And yeah, that's pretty
much-- that's what--

00:21:19.113 --> 00:21:19.655
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:21:19.655 --> 00:21:22.321
PROFESSOR: You guys are supposed
to do a 10-minute presentation,

00:21:22.321 --> 00:21:23.310
so if you can just go--

00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:23.690
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:21:23.690 --> 00:21:24.320
Real quick.

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:27.300
OK, so while Juan and Sid were
doing off-grid interviews,

00:21:27.300 --> 00:21:30.102
Liz and I did some interviews
at the different homes

00:21:30.102 --> 00:21:31.560
of the women, and
we also had a day

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:33.060
where we cooked
with the women, just

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:35.490
to gauge their
usage of charcoal,

00:21:35.490 --> 00:21:37.860
whether they're using it
or not, and reasons why,

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:41.500
and just to really get
an insider's perspective.

00:21:41.500 --> 00:21:45.120
So we found out that most people
aren't using the charcoal.

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:47.130
They prefer to cook
with wood over charcoal,

00:21:47.130 --> 00:21:49.160
basically because they
can gather it for free.

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:50.990
They have to pay to
buy the charcoal.

00:21:50.990 --> 00:21:52.740
They don't have to go
to Ocotal to buy it.

00:21:55.440 --> 00:21:59.160
And they say tortillas are a
very big part of their diet.

00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:01.410
They get up early every
morning, go to the [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:01.410 --> 00:22:03.480
to grind the corn, go
through this process,

00:22:03.480 --> 00:22:04.810
and they make tortillas
every single day.

00:22:04.810 --> 00:22:06.435
So it's a very huge
part of their diet.

00:22:06.435 --> 00:22:10.200
And they found that the
charcoal doesn't burn hot enough

00:22:10.200 --> 00:22:13.380
to make the tortillas, so
they prefer using wood.

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:15.420
And also, on their
adobe stoves, you

00:22:15.420 --> 00:22:18.180
can cook multiple
things at once, which

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:21.430
is also why they prefer wood.

00:22:21.430 --> 00:22:26.425
So this was a good
inside look at why

00:22:26.425 --> 00:22:27.550
they're not using charcoal.

00:22:27.550 --> 00:22:30.360
And we also saw, when we were
cooking with one of the women,

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:33.900
that she kept on using
tinders from the wood fire--

00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:36.690
from that wood fire and she's
dumping it into the charcoal

00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:37.675
to try to light it.

00:22:37.675 --> 00:22:39.550
She was having a really
hard time doing that.

00:22:39.550 --> 00:22:44.700
And a very small side project is
just better charcoal education.

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:49.295
And I think it's going to
be really important to make

00:22:49.295 --> 00:22:51.420
this charcoal affordable
so that they will see this

00:22:51.420 --> 00:22:55.080
as an option over wood.

00:22:55.080 --> 00:22:57.060
Another thing we surveyed
the families about

00:22:57.060 --> 00:22:59.070
was their electricity usage.

00:22:59.070 --> 00:23:03.210
And we noticed that most people,
if they had solar panels,

00:23:03.210 --> 00:23:05.640
they were bought
using green hours.

00:23:05.640 --> 00:23:10.050
And because these
families don't--

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:12.990
they don't want to pay the
extra amount for an inverter,

00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:17.860
so other solar panels are used
for lighting in their homes.

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:20.520
Some families are lucky enough
to pay for-- to have enough

00:23:20.520 --> 00:23:22.620
money to pay for
grid electricity,

00:23:22.620 --> 00:23:25.500
which they use to
charge cell phones and--

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:32.130
some families have
TVs and radios.

00:23:32.130 --> 00:23:35.580
Other families that are only
dependent on solar panel

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:37.860
energy, who also
have cell phones,

00:23:37.860 --> 00:23:39.690
we saw go to their
friends' houses

00:23:39.690 --> 00:23:43.140
to charge phones, which
gave us the idea of having

00:23:43.140 --> 00:23:46.050
maybe a community charger,
community solar power--

00:23:46.050 --> 00:23:48.180
yeah, some of
center where we can

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:50.760
invest in various
panels that does

00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:55.285
have an inverter to maybe
charge these cell phones,

00:23:55.285 --> 00:23:57.660
instead of them borrowing
their friends' grid electricity

00:23:57.660 --> 00:24:00.215
and stuff like that.

00:24:00.215 --> 00:24:01.347
Yeah.

00:24:01.347 --> 00:24:02.930
I don't know if
there's anything else.

00:24:02.930 --> 00:24:06.260
Oh, and these are some of
the homes we stayed in.

00:24:06.260 --> 00:24:08.780
And we had a good time
staying with them,

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:12.830
and learned a lot just from
living their everyday routines

00:24:12.830 --> 00:24:14.680
and stuff like that.

00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:15.680
I think that's about it.

00:24:15.680 --> 00:24:18.950
If anyone else has
anything to add--

00:24:18.950 --> 00:24:22.350
AUDIENCE: Any questions?

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:25.690
Ideas, comments?

00:24:25.690 --> 00:24:27.580
AUDIENCE: We have
a lot of projects.

00:24:27.580 --> 00:24:27.835
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:24:27.835 --> 00:24:29.260
So today you guys are
going to have to--

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:29.890
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:24:29.890 --> 00:24:32.330
PROFESSOR: --work hard
to narrow them down.

00:24:32.330 --> 00:24:35.210
[INAUDIBLE]

00:24:35.210 --> 00:24:36.855
AUDIENCE: Sorry for
going over time.

00:24:36.855 --> 00:24:37.730
PROFESSOR: Thank you.

00:24:37.730 --> 00:24:38.438
AUDIENCE: Thanks.

00:24:38.438 --> 00:24:40.415
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:42.597 --> 00:24:43.680
AUDIENCE: So hi, everyone.

00:24:43.680 --> 00:24:48.470
We were working with ASAPROSAR
in Santa Ana, El Salvador.

00:24:48.470 --> 00:24:50.990
And while we were there, we
worked on two main projects

00:24:50.990 --> 00:24:53.570
just to show them and work
with them to demonstrate.

00:24:53.570 --> 00:24:55.783
We did three charcoal
burns, and we

00:24:55.783 --> 00:24:57.200
showed three
different communities

00:24:57.200 --> 00:25:00.050
how to make corn
shellers for themselves--

00:25:00.050 --> 00:25:02.773
so bringing down a project
that had already been done.

00:25:02.773 --> 00:25:04.940
We're just going to run
through some of our pictures

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:08.490
from charcoal and
explain how that one.

00:25:08.490 --> 00:25:10.580
So at first, they didn't
have an oil barrel

00:25:10.580 --> 00:25:12.410
when we went down
there, so we had

00:25:12.410 --> 00:25:16.190
to go to the cooperative
in one of the communities

00:25:16.190 --> 00:25:19.250
and find an oil barrel, first
of all, and then find someone

00:25:19.250 --> 00:25:21.140
to cut the holes for us.

00:25:21.140 --> 00:25:26.030
So Miguel was kind enough
to drill our holes.

00:25:26.030 --> 00:25:30.913
And then we brought it to
the community, and we just--

00:25:30.913 --> 00:25:32.330
we did three
different communities

00:25:32.330 --> 00:25:34.970
on three different days.

00:25:34.970 --> 00:25:36.980
And all three communities
that we went to

00:25:36.980 --> 00:25:38.450
were really eager to help.

00:25:38.450 --> 00:25:40.920
They all wanted to
be a part of it.

00:25:40.920 --> 00:25:43.400
This particular
guy right here was

00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:45.980
part of a community that was
looking at charcoal more of--

00:25:45.980 --> 00:25:50.567
as an economic goal, I guess.

00:25:50.567 --> 00:25:52.400
They wanted to sell the
charcoal, as opposed

00:25:52.400 --> 00:25:55.310
to using it, because they had
just acquired new stoves where

00:25:55.310 --> 00:25:58.400
they were using wood that
were outside, so they're

00:25:58.400 --> 00:26:00.860
better for ventilation.

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:05.060
So these are just some of
the pictures from that.

00:26:05.060 --> 00:26:08.330
They were all really
excited about the charcoal.

00:26:08.330 --> 00:26:10.820
But the first day, our
burn didn't go so well.

00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:16.960
We learned that using pretty
wet sugar cane wasn't great.

00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:19.460
They hadn't dried up very well,
and sugar cane to start with

00:26:19.460 --> 00:26:21.710
was a lot more dense than
some of the material we

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:23.750
had used when we burned here.

00:26:23.750 --> 00:26:25.887
And we just didn't let
it burn long enough.

00:26:25.887 --> 00:26:27.470
But it was also good
for the community

00:26:27.470 --> 00:26:30.530
to see that, if they didn't
let it burn long enough, then

00:26:30.530 --> 00:26:32.436
they wouldn't get
good charcoal from it.

00:26:32.436 --> 00:26:33.644
Did you want to add anything?

00:26:33.644 --> 00:26:36.170
AUDIENCE: And another
thing we realized

00:26:36.170 --> 00:26:38.780
is that, when-- the first day,
when we grated the yuca, we did

00:26:38.780 --> 00:26:42.410
it in relatively bigger pieces,
so it was still pretty dry,

00:26:42.410 --> 00:26:45.140
didn't release any
of, I guess, the juice

00:26:45.140 --> 00:26:46.620
or whatever it has in it.

00:26:46.620 --> 00:26:50.330
So when we ground it up,
it was still really dry,

00:26:50.330 --> 00:26:52.940
and so when we added it to
the water to make the binder,

00:26:52.940 --> 00:26:56.180
we had to use so
much yuca to get it

00:26:56.180 --> 00:26:57.736
to have that right consistency.

00:27:01.370 --> 00:27:03.380
The one yuca didn't make
a whole lot of binder,

00:27:03.380 --> 00:27:06.435
so we learned to put it
in smaller pieces to make.

00:27:06.435 --> 00:27:08.060
AUDIENCE: You can
see how thick it was.

00:27:08.060 --> 00:27:08.630
This was the first day.

00:27:08.630 --> 00:27:09.797
AUDIENCE: It's really thick.

00:27:09.797 --> 00:27:11.630
AUDIENCE: I think
it's important to note

00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:14.600
that, in all communities,
people were--

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:18.260
some people were somewhat
familiar with charcoal.

00:27:18.260 --> 00:27:20.300
Sometimes they had no idea.

00:27:20.300 --> 00:27:23.605
But it was really
nice that everybody--

00:27:23.605 --> 00:27:24.980
I guess we kind
of expected that,

00:27:24.980 --> 00:27:26.990
but everybody worked
with us, and they came up

00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:28.157
with their own little ideas.

00:27:30.830 --> 00:27:33.080
I guess the two tour guides--

00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:35.300
not tour guides--
that's a bad word--

00:27:35.300 --> 00:27:38.930
the two people helping
us from the organization,

00:27:38.930 --> 00:27:41.570
from ASAPROSAR,
were very helpful,

00:27:41.570 --> 00:27:43.890
and they got really into it.

00:27:43.890 --> 00:27:45.770
And so for the last
one, we actually

00:27:45.770 --> 00:27:47.840
asked them to do
the demonstration,

00:27:47.840 --> 00:27:50.355
and we were checking in and
making sure everything's OK.

00:27:50.355 --> 00:27:51.980
So that worked out
really well in terms

00:27:51.980 --> 00:27:57.920
of involving the community
actually to keep doing this,

00:27:57.920 --> 00:28:00.260
because they're going
to keep showing people

00:28:00.260 --> 00:28:03.600
in different communities,
so it was important for us

00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:06.155
to involve them a
lot in the process.

00:28:06.155 --> 00:28:07.530
AUDIENCE: They
got it down really

00:28:07.530 --> 00:28:12.450
fast, so by the third day,
when we let them run it, we--

00:28:12.450 --> 00:28:15.070
it was all in Spanish and we
couldn't follow most of it,

00:28:15.070 --> 00:28:18.360
so we would ask our translator,
Juan Carlos, who was working

00:28:18.360 --> 00:28:19.920
with us, and one
of the two people

00:28:19.920 --> 00:28:23.802
who was working
in the community--

00:28:23.802 --> 00:28:26.010
we asked him, oh, did you
remember to tell them this?

00:28:26.010 --> 00:28:27.330
He was like, yeah, yeah,
yeah, yeah, I got it.

00:28:27.330 --> 00:28:27.830
I got it.

00:28:27.830 --> 00:28:29.288
AUDIENCE: He was
such a help to us.

00:28:29.288 --> 00:28:30.660
AUDIENCE: He was a total pro.

00:28:30.660 --> 00:28:31.980
So it was really good.

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:33.810
It's really exciting
to know that they'll

00:28:33.810 --> 00:28:36.690
have the know-how
and the confidence

00:28:36.690 --> 00:28:40.530
to really accurately
tell more communities how

00:28:40.530 --> 00:28:41.810
to make the charcoal.

00:28:41.810 --> 00:28:42.810
AUDIENCE: This Is Elena.

00:28:42.810 --> 00:28:44.400
She was one of the
other community partners

00:28:44.400 --> 00:28:45.370
that we worked with.

00:28:45.370 --> 00:28:48.100
She was really into the
motivational aspect of it,

00:28:48.100 --> 00:28:50.070
so she'd always be
like, [SPANISH]..

00:28:50.070 --> 00:28:50.880
You can do it.

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:53.467
She kept encouraging the
people in the community that--

00:28:53.467 --> 00:28:55.050
who are a little
more nervous about it

00:28:55.050 --> 00:28:57.120
or who are more depressed
about their living

00:28:57.120 --> 00:28:59.250
conditions, that they
could keep moving forward.

00:28:59.250 --> 00:29:01.125
So she was really excited
about the charcoal.

00:29:06.150 --> 00:29:11.980
[INAUDIBLE]

00:29:11.980 --> 00:29:14.770
AUDIENCE: It was a lot of
fun that pretty much everyone

00:29:14.770 --> 00:29:17.680
in the community was
really involved with it.

00:29:17.680 --> 00:29:20.080
At least on the last
day, we really sat back

00:29:20.080 --> 00:29:22.240
and didn't really
do anything, and let

00:29:22.240 --> 00:29:23.920
Juan Carlos and Elena
take the reins--

00:29:23.920 --> 00:29:25.170
at least with the instruction.

00:29:25.170 --> 00:29:28.210
But still the entire time
the members of the community

00:29:28.210 --> 00:29:30.680
were really doing everything,
and they jumped right in.

00:29:30.680 --> 00:29:32.770
No one was really very hesitant.

00:29:32.770 --> 00:29:36.790
And there was something a
little bit for everyone to do.

00:29:36.790 --> 00:29:38.470
When we crushed up
the charcoal, there

00:29:38.470 --> 00:29:40.630
were a lot of kids in
the area, so they just

00:29:40.630 --> 00:29:43.000
went to town stomping
on the rice bags.

00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:44.440
It was really a lot of fun.

00:29:47.050 --> 00:29:50.530
The person that was-- drove
us to the [INAUDIBLE] what was

00:29:50.530 --> 00:29:51.210
that--

00:29:51.210 --> 00:29:52.030
AUDIENCE: Julio--

00:29:52.030 --> 00:29:56.590
AUDIENCE: Julio-- one day
he sat there and just made

00:29:56.590 --> 00:29:58.680
charcoal briquette after
charcoal briquette.

00:29:58.680 --> 00:30:01.510
He was like, oh, look
at the ones I've made.

00:30:01.510 --> 00:30:05.300
Everyone was really excited
about it, so that was nice.

00:30:05.300 --> 00:30:07.475
AUDIENCE: So with
the corn shellers,

00:30:07.475 --> 00:30:10.970
[INAUDIBLE] had gone down, and
some of the communities had

00:30:10.970 --> 00:30:11.510
seen--

00:30:11.510 --> 00:30:13.370
or had heard about
using cans that they

00:30:13.370 --> 00:30:15.788
had to make the corn shellers.

00:30:15.788 --> 00:30:17.330
So we had never
heard of that, but we

00:30:17.330 --> 00:30:18.540
were trying to go with it.

00:30:18.540 --> 00:30:20.660
And the first day
that we went out,

00:30:20.660 --> 00:30:23.540
we had to experiment with
using pliers and the cans

00:30:23.540 --> 00:30:25.050
that they had.

00:30:25.050 --> 00:30:26.895
So this was one
of the communities

00:30:26.895 --> 00:30:28.770
that-- they were just
playing around with it.

00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:31.790
And it was almost good in
a way that we didn't really

00:30:31.790 --> 00:30:33.440
know how to do it
ourselves perfectly,

00:30:33.440 --> 00:30:36.920
because it allowed them to
experiment themselves with it.

00:30:36.920 --> 00:30:40.130
They found the best ways
that they wanted to make it.

00:30:40.130 --> 00:30:44.340
They're using little
6-ounce jalapeno cans.

00:30:44.340 --> 00:30:47.140
So these are someone the
woman playing around with it.

00:30:47.140 --> 00:30:48.780
And then we also
did show them how

00:30:48.780 --> 00:30:52.928
to use the jig with the
sheet metal and the hammer.

00:30:52.928 --> 00:30:54.720
One of the things that
we ran into, though,

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:57.960
was one of the communities
didn't have pliers,

00:30:57.960 --> 00:31:01.530
so we had to show them how to do
the whole thing with a hammer.

00:31:01.530 --> 00:31:03.840
And eventually,
by the third day,

00:31:03.840 --> 00:31:07.590
we came up with this idea of
having a variable corn sheller.

00:31:07.590 --> 00:31:12.060
So we didn't actually close
it, and we rounded the edges

00:31:12.060 --> 00:31:14.070
so it was just like--
could hold it in your hand

00:31:14.070 --> 00:31:15.810
and adjust it to the
size of the corn,

00:31:15.810 --> 00:31:19.420
because the corn varied a lot
from different communities.

00:31:19.420 --> 00:31:24.120
So that was really helpful, and
this girl was really into it.

00:31:24.120 --> 00:31:26.760
AUDIENCE: It was nice
when we found the way

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:31.230
to do it without fully
attaching the ends so

00:31:31.230 --> 00:31:35.430
that, since a lot of the
corn were different sizes,

00:31:35.430 --> 00:31:37.950
you would see a lot of the
members of the community

00:31:37.950 --> 00:31:39.810
use one corn shell
and try to shell it,

00:31:39.810 --> 00:31:42.270
and it didn't work because
it wasn't the right size.

00:31:42.270 --> 00:31:44.130
So they'd toss it out
and try another one.

00:31:44.130 --> 00:31:46.773
And you could see it
was kind of frustrating.

00:31:46.773 --> 00:31:48.940
Who wants a corn sheller
that doesn't actually work?

00:31:48.940 --> 00:31:51.000
So once we figured
out how to do it

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:52.950
with just rounding
the edges so you could

00:31:52.950 --> 00:31:57.690
just the-- how tightly
you gripped it made it

00:31:57.690 --> 00:31:59.880
work for every single corncob.

00:31:59.880 --> 00:32:01.005
It was a lot more exciting.

00:32:01.005 --> 00:32:04.560
And when we first showed
it to them that day,

00:32:04.560 --> 00:32:07.350
the lady tried it, and
promptly put it in her pocket,

00:32:07.350 --> 00:32:10.440
and went around with
the rest of the day.

00:32:10.440 --> 00:32:11.670
It was amazing.

00:32:11.670 --> 00:32:14.230
AUDIENCE: The third community
we went to actually--

00:32:14.230 --> 00:32:15.990
we went and toured
houses and looked

00:32:15.990 --> 00:32:18.110
at stoves in between,
when we had first

00:32:18.110 --> 00:32:19.360
showing them how to make them.

00:32:19.360 --> 00:32:21.060
And we came back
a few hours later,

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:22.860
and they were still all
sitting there making them.

00:32:22.860 --> 00:32:24.430
They had been doing
it for hours on end.

00:32:24.430 --> 00:32:25.060
They loved it.

00:32:28.860 --> 00:32:31.360
Now we're going to talk to you
about some potential projects

00:32:31.360 --> 00:32:32.170
we came up with.

00:32:35.047 --> 00:32:36.630
AUDIENCE: I guess,
as an introduction,

00:32:36.630 --> 00:32:38.795
because our trip was--

00:32:38.795 --> 00:32:39.670
we just went with it.

00:32:39.670 --> 00:32:42.670
We really didn't know the exact
projects they were working on,

00:32:42.670 --> 00:32:44.580
so we took a lot of notes.

00:32:44.580 --> 00:32:46.710
And there's so many
different issues

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:48.690
that they're dealing
with, and a lot of them

00:32:48.690 --> 00:32:52.320
are not energy-related, per se.

00:32:52.320 --> 00:32:56.850
But we took notes on
all of them, and we'll--

00:32:56.850 --> 00:32:59.880
I guess we can pass that
on to other D-Lab classes.

00:32:59.880 --> 00:33:05.360
But we picked four
as the main projects

00:33:05.360 --> 00:33:08.570
that we can focus
on in the class.

00:33:08.570 --> 00:33:15.220
And I guess the main
ideas were lighting

00:33:15.220 --> 00:33:23.020
optimization, ventilation, a
charcoal insert, and a rice

00:33:23.020 --> 00:33:23.680
husking--

00:33:23.680 --> 00:33:28.702
modifying a mill
to do rice husking.

00:33:28.702 --> 00:33:29.785
How do we want to do this?

00:33:29.785 --> 00:33:30.430
AUDIENCE: I can just--

00:33:30.430 --> 00:33:30.760
AUDIENCE: Go ahead.

00:33:30.760 --> 00:33:31.490
Yeah.

00:33:31.490 --> 00:33:33.615
AUDIENCE: So one of the
big problems in El Salvador

00:33:33.615 --> 00:33:35.810
is there's only 3%
forest right now.

00:33:35.810 --> 00:33:37.810
They're running into a
lot of issues with people

00:33:37.810 --> 00:33:40.100
using all the wood for cooking.

00:33:40.100 --> 00:33:41.830
So although some of
the families wanted

00:33:41.830 --> 00:33:44.082
to use the charcoal to
sell it and make money off

00:33:44.082 --> 00:33:45.790
of it, other families
we're looking at it

00:33:45.790 --> 00:33:48.915
as a fuel source for
themselves for cooking.

00:33:48.915 --> 00:33:50.290
But one of the
problems with that

00:33:50.290 --> 00:33:52.390
is that they were--
the ASAPROSAR team

00:33:52.390 --> 00:33:55.900
was working with the communities
to build new stoves outside,

00:33:55.900 --> 00:33:59.600
and those stoves weren't
optimal for charcoal.

00:33:59.600 --> 00:34:01.643
This is a picture
of one of them.

00:34:01.643 --> 00:34:03.310
If you can see, it
has the nice chimney,

00:34:03.310 --> 00:34:06.100
so it ventilates really
well, and it's outside.

00:34:06.100 --> 00:34:09.010
So it burns really
well with wood.

00:34:09.010 --> 00:34:12.429
So we were thinking along
the lines of something

00:34:12.429 --> 00:34:15.550
that we could insert into
one of these types of stoves

00:34:15.550 --> 00:34:18.339
that would make it be
able to use charcoal.

00:34:20.920 --> 00:34:25.239
A lot of the materials
that they have there--

00:34:25.239 --> 00:34:27.100
we thought about
sheet metal, and we

00:34:27.100 --> 00:34:29.260
thought about
different ways to make

00:34:29.260 --> 00:34:31.150
a kind of fryer basket thing.

00:34:31.150 --> 00:34:36.645
But sheet metal is too expensive
for their living means, so we--

00:34:36.645 --> 00:34:38.020
for our project,
we were thinking

00:34:38.020 --> 00:34:41.590
about something with the cans
that we had talked about they

00:34:41.590 --> 00:34:44.020
use for corn shellers.

00:34:44.020 --> 00:34:47.221
They can buy them in the
market, and they're--

00:34:47.221 --> 00:34:49.179
they have food in them
when they come with them

00:34:49.179 --> 00:34:50.860
so that they can use the food.

00:34:50.860 --> 00:34:53.170
We were thinking about
potentially making the cans--

00:34:53.170 --> 00:34:55.885
just forming it and weaving
it into some kind of charcoal

00:34:55.885 --> 00:34:57.760
basket that we could
insert into these stoves

00:34:57.760 --> 00:34:59.927
so they could cook with the
charcoal that they make.

00:35:02.670 --> 00:35:06.090
AUDIENCE: Something important,
I guess, to add for all of these

00:35:06.090 --> 00:35:09.700
was that I think all
three communities--

00:35:09.700 --> 00:35:12.990
and I think one was a little
bit better than the other ones--

00:35:12.990 --> 00:35:17.490
but they really didn't
have any resources.

00:35:17.490 --> 00:35:21.180
We spoke to a lot of
the people, and it

00:35:21.180 --> 00:35:23.620
seemed like most are women.

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:25.380
Most of them are not working.

00:35:25.380 --> 00:35:28.920
So it was really difficult
to ask questions like, well,

00:35:28.920 --> 00:35:30.930
can you afford buying
something, because most

00:35:30.930 --> 00:35:32.805
of the time they don't
really have any money.

00:35:35.640 --> 00:35:37.650
I guess the biggest
challenge is try

00:35:37.650 --> 00:35:41.580
to find materials that
already exist, or maybe are

00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:42.600
part of the trash.

00:35:42.600 --> 00:35:48.820
And we spoke to people
trying to figure out,

00:35:48.820 --> 00:35:50.920
what are the type of
things that they can find?

00:35:50.920 --> 00:35:54.280
And I guess the cans
is one of the things--

00:35:54.280 --> 00:35:56.200
also the chips bags.

00:35:56.200 --> 00:35:59.650
Empty chips bags was
mentioned as something that--

00:35:59.650 --> 00:36:02.980
it could be found near schools.

00:36:02.980 --> 00:36:06.190
Plastic bottles, any
size of plastic bottles

00:36:06.190 --> 00:36:08.230
can be used-- so
things like that.

00:36:08.230 --> 00:36:12.700
We were trying to see if we can
somehow incorporate any of this

00:36:12.700 --> 00:36:15.220
into the design so
that it's not really

00:36:15.220 --> 00:36:17.680
something that you can go
buy, because they can't really

00:36:17.680 --> 00:36:20.240
go buy it.

00:36:20.240 --> 00:36:25.097
AUDIENCE: So you want to
talk about the [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:25.097 --> 00:36:26.500
AUDIENCE: I can do it.

00:36:26.500 --> 00:36:30.250
So the second problem that
we identified as something we

00:36:30.250 --> 00:36:34.048
could address was
rice dehusking.

00:36:34.048 --> 00:36:35.590
Some of the communities
were starting

00:36:35.590 --> 00:36:40.210
to experiment with integrating
rice into their diet,

00:36:40.210 --> 00:36:43.030
as well as their
standard corn and beans

00:36:43.030 --> 00:36:45.730
for additional
nutrients, or at least

00:36:45.730 --> 00:36:48.085
having it as another source of--

00:36:50.950 --> 00:36:52.570
to sell, potentially.

00:36:52.570 --> 00:36:56.980
But currently, the problem
was, if the-- they could either

00:36:56.980 --> 00:36:59.740
shell all of the
rice by hand, or they

00:36:59.740 --> 00:37:04.250
could take it to a mill that
was about three hours away.

00:37:04.250 --> 00:37:07.328
And they had to pay about
250 to get the bag dehusked,

00:37:07.328 --> 00:37:08.620
and then they had to come back.

00:37:08.620 --> 00:37:11.030
So it was a very long
and tedious process.

00:37:11.030 --> 00:37:14.590
They [? spent ?]
well over their--

00:37:14.590 --> 00:37:19.210
a couple days' salary
or work just doing this,

00:37:19.210 --> 00:37:22.030
and it's really not very
economical, and it's very hard.

00:37:22.030 --> 00:37:26.650
So they experimented
with the mill to grind--

00:37:26.650 --> 00:37:33.130
that they used to use to
grind corn to dehusk the rice.

00:37:33.130 --> 00:37:35.620
To make it softer,
they had-- some

00:37:35.620 --> 00:37:40.060
of the communities had put
rubber in between the pieces

00:37:40.060 --> 00:37:42.130
of the mill.

00:37:42.130 --> 00:37:43.750
But they found
that the rubber was

00:37:43.750 --> 00:37:48.950
turning the rice kernels
black, which is not good.

00:37:48.950 --> 00:37:51.730
So we bought a couple
mills and experimented

00:37:51.730 --> 00:37:56.890
with other materials, which
included a lighter color rubber

00:37:56.890 --> 00:37:59.230
material and leather.

00:37:59.230 --> 00:38:02.200
So it was more organic material
to place in between it.

00:38:02.200 --> 00:38:04.540
And we tried that
Saturday before we left,

00:38:04.540 --> 00:38:07.000
and we had a decent
amount of success with it.

00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:08.860
There has to be some
more fine tuning,

00:38:08.860 --> 00:38:11.680
obviously, and probably more--

00:38:11.680 --> 00:38:17.170
to potentially find some
more optimal materials

00:38:17.170 --> 00:38:19.930
to use as the barrier,
but from what we found,

00:38:19.930 --> 00:38:22.450
it's definitely got
potential, and it

00:38:22.450 --> 00:38:25.077
shouldn't be too hard
of a project to tackle.

00:38:25.077 --> 00:38:27.160
AUDIENCE: And that's
something I think that's also

00:38:27.160 --> 00:38:28.480
supported by ASAPROSAR.

00:38:28.480 --> 00:38:33.840
They're supplying these
mills to some communities

00:38:33.840 --> 00:38:37.580
so that they can use it
almost as a microeconomy

00:38:37.580 --> 00:38:39.700
to make money.

00:38:39.700 --> 00:38:42.960
AUDIENCE: The insert goes right
in between these two pieces.

00:38:42.960 --> 00:38:45.773
They're both, I
guess, ridged, so they

00:38:45.773 --> 00:38:47.190
grind against each
other, but when

00:38:47.190 --> 00:38:48.810
you have something
in between, it

00:38:48.810 --> 00:38:53.490
stops it from pulling
the rice totally apart.

00:38:53.490 --> 00:38:57.123
AUDIENCE: For the
lighting, there

00:38:57.123 --> 00:38:58.540
were a couple of
different issues.

00:38:58.540 --> 00:39:02.430
One was electrical lighting
and one was day lighting.

00:39:02.430 --> 00:39:06.870
For electrical lighting, some
communities-- some people

00:39:06.870 --> 00:39:08.460
had lights inside.

00:39:08.460 --> 00:39:11.340
Some had lights that worked
and some that didn't work,

00:39:11.340 --> 00:39:12.500
so it was--

00:39:12.500 --> 00:39:13.230
it varied.

00:39:13.230 --> 00:39:15.060
The conditions varied.

00:39:15.060 --> 00:39:18.940
However, the families
we asked about,

00:39:18.940 --> 00:39:21.220
is the light bulb enough--

00:39:21.220 --> 00:39:24.310
couple of families
said yeah for--

00:39:24.310 --> 00:39:27.190
at night, they had
one room in a way,

00:39:27.190 --> 00:39:30.940
and that it was sufficient
to light that room.

00:39:30.940 --> 00:39:37.155
In terms of day lighting-- so
the houses were completely--

00:39:37.155 --> 00:39:38.030
AUDIENCE: Very dark--

00:39:38.030 --> 00:39:45.220
AUDIENCE: Very dark-- some of
the questions we asked is-- are

00:39:45.220 --> 00:39:47.710
why are they so dark?

00:39:47.710 --> 00:39:51.130
And is that more of-- it's
expensive, or do you need

00:39:51.130 --> 00:39:52.210
more light?

00:39:52.210 --> 00:39:54.700
And so most of them
are one or two rooms,

00:39:54.700 --> 00:39:58.600
and they had one window,
the whole house was.

00:39:58.600 --> 00:40:00.610
Sometimes they didn't
even have a window.

00:40:00.610 --> 00:40:03.320
And we found out that there were
a couple of different issues.

00:40:03.320 --> 00:40:05.110
One was money.

00:40:05.110 --> 00:40:08.110
Because the houses were
made with adobe bricks,

00:40:08.110 --> 00:40:10.510
in order to leave--

00:40:10.510 --> 00:40:13.000
it seems simple that you just
leave an opening so that you

00:40:13.000 --> 00:40:14.480
actually save on material.

00:40:14.480 --> 00:40:19.030
However, for safety,
and also for hurricanes,

00:40:19.030 --> 00:40:20.920
they have to close that opening.

00:40:20.920 --> 00:40:24.260
And to close that opening,
if they use wood or metal,

00:40:24.260 --> 00:40:27.280
it costs anywhere
around $25 per window,

00:40:27.280 --> 00:40:29.260
whereas the bricks are $0.30.

00:40:29.260 --> 00:40:32.920
So that's $30 versus $25--

00:40:32.920 --> 00:40:35.390
is a big difference.

00:40:35.390 --> 00:40:38.710
Another issue they
said was some of them

00:40:38.710 --> 00:40:43.420
didn't actually want more light
because of privacy reasons.

00:40:43.420 --> 00:40:46.060
Parents and kids sometimes
are in the same room,

00:40:46.060 --> 00:40:48.910
so they were kind of joking
around, but they said,

00:40:48.910 --> 00:40:52.630
we can't do anything because,
if you can see things--

00:40:52.630 --> 00:40:54.818
kids are always aware of
what the parents are doing,

00:40:54.818 --> 00:40:55.860
and the other way around.

00:40:55.860 --> 00:41:00.970
So it was more of
a privacy issue.

00:41:00.970 --> 00:41:05.020
However, day lighting
could be improved,

00:41:05.020 --> 00:41:08.760
or it would be great if
they have more light for all

00:41:08.760 --> 00:41:10.680
of the tasks they're working.

00:41:10.680 --> 00:41:14.280
And some of the ideas
we had were, again,

00:41:14.280 --> 00:41:17.980
using existing materials.

00:41:17.980 --> 00:41:23.220
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:23.220 --> 00:41:27.080
AUDIENCE: We met a Peace
Corps person from Texas who

00:41:27.080 --> 00:41:30.710
used this in his house, and he
said that the roof was leaking,

00:41:30.710 --> 00:41:34.430
so he just put a plastic
bottle instead of one

00:41:34.430 --> 00:41:35.780
of the roof tiles.

00:41:35.780 --> 00:41:39.230
And that was actually, we
thought, a really great idea,

00:41:39.230 --> 00:41:41.310
because plastic
bottles are available.

00:41:41.310 --> 00:41:45.260
And if we can find a way to
maybe cut the bottle in half,

00:41:45.260 --> 00:41:49.100
and try to mimic the
shape of the roof tiles,

00:41:49.100 --> 00:41:54.570
and incorporate some plastic
bottles within the roof

00:41:54.570 --> 00:41:58.800
so that stops the rain, but
allows light to come in,

00:41:58.800 --> 00:41:59.880
that was one idea.

00:41:59.880 --> 00:42:03.600
Another possibility was there
was a gap between the wall

00:42:03.600 --> 00:42:07.890
in the roof with about
6 inches in most homes,

00:42:07.890 --> 00:42:10.435
and even though that's
a lot more challenging,

00:42:10.435 --> 00:42:12.810
we were trying to think of,
is there anything that we can

00:42:12.810 --> 00:42:16.770
introduce some a surface that
can catch some of the light

00:42:16.770 --> 00:42:19.230
from the outside and
bring it inside--

00:42:19.230 --> 00:42:22.550
something like the chips bags,
which is kind of like aluminum

00:42:22.550 --> 00:42:24.470
foil--

00:42:24.470 --> 00:42:26.190
to redirect it?

00:42:26.190 --> 00:42:28.850
However, that's challenging,
because the roofs--

00:42:28.850 --> 00:42:30.740
or the ceilings were--

00:42:30.740 --> 00:42:35.580
and walls were completely
black from the smoke

00:42:35.580 --> 00:42:39.350
of the cooking inside, which,
I guess, leads to the last--

00:42:39.350 --> 00:42:44.550
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] so I guess
the last project we were really

00:42:44.550 --> 00:42:46.440
working on was the ventilation.

00:42:46.440 --> 00:42:49.380
So like [INAUDIBLE]
said, a lot of the homes

00:42:49.380 --> 00:42:52.140
still had the stoves
inside, and a lot of them

00:42:52.140 --> 00:42:54.450
were simply the
three brick stoves.

00:42:54.450 --> 00:42:57.300
They just stuck the wood under
it, and proceeded to cook.

00:42:57.300 --> 00:43:03.000
And it essentially charred
the walls completely black.

00:43:03.000 --> 00:43:04.410
A lot of it was covered in soot.

00:43:04.410 --> 00:43:11.280
And with the financial problem
of trying to even incorporate

00:43:11.280 --> 00:43:14.040
windows into the housing,
which-- so much more expensive,

00:43:14.040 --> 00:43:18.420
that we really had a
difficult time trying

00:43:18.420 --> 00:43:21.100
to think of ways to
improve the ventilation,

00:43:21.100 --> 00:43:23.040
especially considering
the fact that a lot

00:43:23.040 --> 00:43:27.060
of the members of the
community don't make money.

00:43:27.060 --> 00:43:29.350
They just live off the
land that they have.

00:43:29.350 --> 00:43:34.710
So the idea of buying materials
is pretty much impossible.

00:43:34.710 --> 00:43:39.180
So one of the thoughts
we had, other than

00:43:39.180 --> 00:43:41.470
improving the stove so that
they're more efficient,

00:43:41.470 --> 00:43:43.450
trying to get them
to move outside--

00:43:43.450 --> 00:43:48.150
which they have problems
with, since the stoves

00:43:48.150 --> 00:43:50.770
they have right now
are not very efficient.

00:43:50.770 --> 00:43:52.860
They have to make
sure they close off

00:43:52.860 --> 00:43:55.050
the entire area
for wind and rain,

00:43:55.050 --> 00:43:58.290
because they're already losing
efficiency just with the stoves

00:43:58.290 --> 00:44:00.300
they're using.

00:44:00.300 --> 00:44:02.340
So a lot of them wouldn't mind--

00:44:02.340 --> 00:44:05.880
if they don't leave
the stove inside,

00:44:05.880 --> 00:44:09.570
they build an entire new
room with a roof and a wall

00:44:09.570 --> 00:44:11.610
outside of the
house to put their.

00:44:11.610 --> 00:44:14.490
AUDIENCE: So this was the start
one of the outside kitchens

00:44:14.490 --> 00:44:16.030
that they're making.

00:44:16.030 --> 00:44:19.932
So they literally close
a section off with walls,

00:44:19.932 --> 00:44:22.140
and then they're going to
probably put something over

00:44:22.140 --> 00:44:23.200
for a roof.

00:44:23.200 --> 00:44:23.700
[INAUDIBLE]

00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:25.075
AUDIENCE: So
essentially, they're

00:44:25.075 --> 00:44:28.140
making an entire new room.

00:44:28.140 --> 00:44:30.150
AUDIENCE: And that's
supported by ASAPROSAR.

00:44:30.150 --> 00:44:35.060
What they're doing is trying to
find families who are in the--

00:44:35.060 --> 00:44:39.860
have the most need in a way,
and they're supplying them

00:44:39.860 --> 00:44:43.000
with sometimes materials.

00:44:43.000 --> 00:44:45.580
They said that the way the
organization works is they

00:44:45.580 --> 00:44:47.878
never actually do it for you.

00:44:47.878 --> 00:44:49.420
They would get a
grant from somewhere

00:44:49.420 --> 00:44:52.070
and actually make
the community do it.

00:44:52.070 --> 00:44:55.750
But these stoves that they're
building outside, or kitchens,

00:44:55.750 --> 00:44:58.110
are not for everybody in a way.

00:44:58.110 --> 00:45:00.110
So there are only certain
families who get them,

00:45:00.110 --> 00:45:02.170
so it's still a
really big issue of,

00:45:02.170 --> 00:45:06.040
how do we deal with
the stoves inside?

00:45:06.040 --> 00:45:11.350
AUDIENCE: So we thought, as
far as a makeshift smoke hood,

00:45:11.350 --> 00:45:16.180
essentially, since there was
the gap in between the house

00:45:16.180 --> 00:45:18.610
and the roof, we thought
that maybe we could make some

00:45:18.610 --> 00:45:21.250
of hood out of the materials
that they already have--

00:45:21.250 --> 00:45:27.340
maybe overused clothes, or even
the chip bags if there was some

00:45:27.340 --> 00:45:33.040
way to sew them together, and
channel the smoke at least up

00:45:33.040 --> 00:45:36.010
into the cracks of the house and
hopefully guide it a little bit

00:45:36.010 --> 00:45:39.460
more outside of the
room that they have--

00:45:39.460 --> 00:45:44.530
because essentially, they
are just one-room huts,

00:45:44.530 --> 00:45:46.810
more or less, so they're--

00:45:46.810 --> 00:45:49.640
with no windows and one doorway.

00:45:49.640 --> 00:45:51.820
So there really isn't
anywhere for the smoke

00:45:51.820 --> 00:45:55.390
to go if it's not-- if it
can't be better channeled out.

00:45:55.390 --> 00:46:00.100
And since it is only one room,
you sleep there, you eat there,

00:46:00.100 --> 00:46:03.700
you cook there, so it's-- the
ventilation is pretty bad.

00:46:03.700 --> 00:46:06.460
And that's one of the things
we really want to work on.

00:46:09.370 --> 00:46:11.180
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:46:11.180 --> 00:46:13.450
AUDIENCE: Ending on a more
positive note, though,

00:46:13.450 --> 00:46:14.715
it was an amazing experience.

00:46:14.715 --> 00:46:16.090
Like we said, all
the communities

00:46:16.090 --> 00:46:19.810
were very encouraging,
and really wanted

00:46:19.810 --> 00:46:22.060
to make these changes.

00:46:22.060 --> 00:46:26.590
There wasn't anyone who
was just the woe is me,

00:46:26.590 --> 00:46:28.760
too depressed to even
try to make the change.

00:46:28.760 --> 00:46:33.130
So it was nice to know that
they were really into me

00:46:33.130 --> 00:46:35.950
making these improvements.

00:46:35.950 --> 00:46:39.520
AUDIENCE: Just to add, something
important was ASAPROSAR--

00:46:39.520 --> 00:46:42.850
the way they work with these
communities is to help them,

00:46:42.850 --> 00:46:45.280
but to also help the land.

00:46:45.280 --> 00:46:48.310
So there was a really
big influence on,

00:46:48.310 --> 00:46:50.650
how can we make some
of these improvements,

00:46:50.650 --> 00:46:55.150
but not hurt the remaining
nature that we have?

00:46:55.150 --> 00:46:58.480
So a lot of the other
issues that we encountered

00:46:58.480 --> 00:47:01.300
were water, latrines--

00:47:01.300 --> 00:47:03.460
how do they make
them without actually

00:47:03.460 --> 00:47:05.980
contaminating the water?

00:47:05.980 --> 00:47:08.760
So I guess part of
these challenges

00:47:08.760 --> 00:47:13.580
is to figure out ways that
don't hurt nature any more

00:47:13.580 --> 00:47:15.920
so than what is
already existing,

00:47:15.920 --> 00:47:18.770
because that's one of the
biggest issues they're

00:47:18.770 --> 00:47:19.674
dealing with there.

00:47:23.247 --> 00:47:24.080
PROFESSOR: OK, guys.

00:47:24.080 --> 00:47:26.430
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:29.250 --> 00:47:32.160
AUDIENCE: OK, so
introduction-wise, we

00:47:32.160 --> 00:47:34.650
were the group that
went with AsoFenix.

00:47:34.650 --> 00:47:38.220
AsoFenix is a group that's
based out of Managua.

00:47:38.220 --> 00:47:40.500
It works with a group called
Green Empowerment that's

00:47:40.500 --> 00:47:42.000
based out of Portland
so it's really

00:47:42.000 --> 00:47:44.460
quite a good collaboration.

00:47:44.460 --> 00:47:47.580
But anyway, they
focus on bringing

00:47:47.580 --> 00:47:50.070
potable water
systems, green energy

00:47:50.070 --> 00:47:52.140
solutions to different towns.

00:47:52.140 --> 00:47:53.580
But the really
cool thing is, when

00:47:53.580 --> 00:47:55.260
they go to implement
these things, they bring

00:47:55.260 --> 00:47:56.677
a lot of infrastructure
with them,

00:47:56.677 --> 00:47:59.273
because oh, we're going to
install a water system-- well,

00:47:59.273 --> 00:48:01.440
you're going to need an
energy source for your pump.

00:48:01.440 --> 00:48:02.670
Well, you're going
to need a room

00:48:02.670 --> 00:48:04.003
to get all your equipment there.

00:48:04.003 --> 00:48:05.850
Well, everyone should
have a clean latrine

00:48:05.850 --> 00:48:08.440
so it doesn't clean--
dirty the water system.

00:48:08.440 --> 00:48:10.440
So they really do a
lot of compound work,

00:48:10.440 --> 00:48:12.810
and that's why it was really
exciting to travel around

00:48:12.810 --> 00:48:16.982
with some of their interns,
see what they do firsthand.

00:48:16.982 --> 00:48:18.690
AUDIENCE: So I guess
we'll just run down.

00:48:18.690 --> 00:48:21.450
We went down there with
four projects in mind,

00:48:21.450 --> 00:48:27.960
and we are going to present
those four projects and where

00:48:27.960 --> 00:48:30.510
we see further action
possibilities for each

00:48:30.510 --> 00:48:31.890
of those projects are.

00:48:31.890 --> 00:48:34.920
So we'll start with the
juice sealer, the bag sealer,

00:48:34.920 --> 00:48:38.340
which was a project from
last year's class, which

00:48:38.340 --> 00:48:40.740
seals a plastic bag for the--

00:48:40.740 --> 00:48:45.780
so that they can preserve juice
that the women are working on

00:48:45.780 --> 00:48:46.630
in the community.

00:48:46.630 --> 00:48:52.020
So we went down-- and this
machine device runs off

00:48:52.020 --> 00:48:54.870
of power from a 12-volt
battery, and we went down,

00:48:54.870 --> 00:49:01.770
and since last year they
have installed and connected

00:49:01.770 --> 00:49:07.180
a micro hydroplane to about half
the houses in the community.

00:49:07.180 --> 00:49:09.360
So this house that we
were staying in actually

00:49:09.360 --> 00:49:13.230
had 120 volts of
power coming out

00:49:13.230 --> 00:49:15.870
of like a normal
outlet in the wall.

00:49:15.870 --> 00:49:19.710
But they did not have
any more batteries

00:49:19.710 --> 00:49:24.780
that were not dead, so we
tried multiple ways to get

00:49:24.780 --> 00:49:27.720
that to work.

00:49:27.720 --> 00:49:30.210
AUDIENCE: Clarification is
coming out of the wall was AC,

00:49:30.210 --> 00:49:34.770
and the device had a solenoid
that did not like AC at all.

00:49:34.770 --> 00:49:35.270
Therefore--

00:49:35.270 --> 00:49:37.510
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] it
really didn't want 120 volts.

00:49:37.510 --> 00:49:37.860
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:49:37.860 --> 00:49:39.235
AUDIENCE: Yeah,
there's that too.

00:49:39.235 --> 00:49:42.420
AUDIENCE: So we tried reflecting
with a corrugated metal

00:49:42.420 --> 00:49:44.280
piece of roofing
onto a solar panel,

00:49:44.280 --> 00:49:47.970
but didn't have
enough current coming

00:49:47.970 --> 00:49:50.490
out of that to run the device.

00:49:50.490 --> 00:49:53.430
And then we also tried
charging a battery--

00:49:53.430 --> 00:49:58.800
a car battery that they had from
before the micro hydroplane,

00:49:58.800 --> 00:50:01.890
but that battery
was completely dead.

00:50:01.890 --> 00:50:04.320
It charged from like
1.5 volts to like

00:50:04.320 --> 00:50:07.230
1.9 volts in three hours.

00:50:07.230 --> 00:50:09.780
So then we strung
up those batteries

00:50:09.780 --> 00:50:14.760
that you saw in the
last picture here

00:50:14.760 --> 00:50:18.420
to get it to work so we could
at least give a demonstration.

00:50:18.420 --> 00:50:21.815
And that was some good
[? compo ?] engineering.

00:50:21.815 --> 00:50:22.440
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:50:22.440 --> 00:50:25.010
AUDIENCE: And so we were
able to give a presentation,

00:50:25.010 --> 00:50:27.210
and they were pretty
excited about being

00:50:27.210 --> 00:50:28.200
able to seal the bags.

00:50:28.200 --> 00:50:32.259
But leaving them a
string of batteries

00:50:32.259 --> 00:50:34.842
on a stick taped together is not
something that's sustainable.

00:50:34.842 --> 00:50:36.690
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:38.940 --> 00:50:42.450
For this project, we
can further develop it

00:50:42.450 --> 00:50:46.950
by making it so that it
can run off of the AC power

00:50:46.950 --> 00:50:48.277
that they have.

00:50:48.277 --> 00:50:49.860
And then we also
found out, after they

00:50:49.860 --> 00:50:51.390
left, that some
houses that aren't

00:50:51.390 --> 00:50:53.490
on the hydro electric grid
still have solar panels

00:50:53.490 --> 00:50:58.860
and charged 12-volt
batteries, so bettering

00:50:58.860 --> 00:51:02.400
the design for this project
to suit that-- because they

00:51:02.400 --> 00:51:05.220
might-- depending on what
we hear back from AsoFenix,

00:51:05.220 --> 00:51:08.040
they might have to take
out the air in the bag

00:51:08.040 --> 00:51:11.250
so that the seal would have
to be a seal for the juice

00:51:11.250 --> 00:51:14.423
airtight and no air in the
bag to completely preserve

00:51:14.423 --> 00:51:15.840
the juice so that
they can sell it

00:51:15.840 --> 00:51:19.680
after the peak orange
harvesting season, when there's

00:51:19.680 --> 00:51:23.240
a market for orange juice.

00:51:23.240 --> 00:51:28.690
So yeah, moving
on to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:51:28.690 --> 00:51:30.850
Sorry, these aren't in order.

00:51:30.850 --> 00:51:31.690
I though it was.

00:51:31.690 --> 00:51:33.370
AUDIENCE: Sure.

00:51:33.370 --> 00:51:35.770
So another cool project
that got to go and see

00:51:35.770 --> 00:51:38.980
was a biodigester.

00:51:38.980 --> 00:51:41.170
Yes, this is the same
one that we went and saw

00:51:41.170 --> 00:51:43.900
in a town called [INAUDIBLE].

00:51:43.900 --> 00:51:46.540
It's hooked up to
the grid, right?

00:51:46.540 --> 00:51:47.390
Yes.

00:51:47.390 --> 00:51:49.870
Yeah, power has
come to [INAUDIBLE],,

00:51:49.870 --> 00:51:52.413
but people still
cook on wood stoves.

00:51:52.413 --> 00:51:53.830
And a great way
to supplement that

00:51:53.830 --> 00:51:57.320
is with methane from
biodigesters like this one.

00:51:57.320 --> 00:51:59.410
So this one belonged
to a household

00:51:59.410 --> 00:52:01.810
where the woman cooked
twice a day on it.

00:52:01.810 --> 00:52:03.100
But it would often run out.

00:52:03.100 --> 00:52:06.290
She'd have really no idea how
much gas it was producing.

00:52:06.290 --> 00:52:08.380
So one of the projects
we went down there with

00:52:08.380 --> 00:52:11.890
was to make some of simple,
yet elegant meter for her

00:52:11.890 --> 00:52:17.465
to tell just how much gas was in
a pressure meter, essentially.

00:52:17.465 --> 00:52:18.340
Do we have a picture?

00:52:18.340 --> 00:52:18.970
Yes.

00:52:18.970 --> 00:52:22.930
OK, so our simple [? compo ?]
engineering solution was

00:52:22.930 --> 00:52:27.490
to take a piece of clear
tubing we had brought,

00:52:27.490 --> 00:52:31.300
mark off some in increments so
you could see whether or not

00:52:31.300 --> 00:52:34.330
the gas coming in
from the tank--

00:52:34.330 --> 00:52:36.910
which is the gray piece
of conduit there--

00:52:36.910 --> 00:52:38.600
would press against the water.

00:52:38.600 --> 00:52:42.580
So basically, you would read
where the water in the tube

00:52:42.580 --> 00:52:45.520
meant the air, and it would
indicate if the pressure had

00:52:45.520 --> 00:52:47.020
risen or fallen.

00:52:47.020 --> 00:52:48.760
Yeah.

00:52:48.760 --> 00:52:52.510
So it's also a pressure
release system,

00:52:52.510 --> 00:52:56.350
which is really important
if the bag gets too--

00:52:56.350 --> 00:52:58.120
you don't want
the bag to get too

00:52:58.120 --> 00:53:00.370
over-filled with gas and the
pressure to get too high.

00:53:00.370 --> 00:53:03.760
So basically, if the pressure
somehow got to the end of that

00:53:03.760 --> 00:53:06.310
6 and 1/2 inches of tube--
which is highly unlikely,

00:53:06.310 --> 00:53:08.080
since she uses it every day--

00:53:08.080 --> 00:53:11.308
the gas would simply just
bubble up to the top.

00:53:11.308 --> 00:53:12.850
However, there were
some difficulties

00:53:12.850 --> 00:53:16.510
that we noticed, like with the
daily feeding this machine--

00:53:16.510 --> 00:53:20.930
or this system, just
basic use of it.

00:53:20.930 --> 00:53:24.880
We hopefully solved that
one with this simple meter.

00:53:24.880 --> 00:53:28.270
But another one was just
it's a very messy task.

00:53:28.270 --> 00:53:31.150
Basically, it's a whole 5-gallon
bucket of manure that has to be

00:53:31.150 --> 00:53:34.930
mixed in a one-part manure,
three-part water mixture,

00:53:34.930 --> 00:53:37.310
and then added, and
then poured through--

00:53:37.310 --> 00:53:39.587
if we go back to the picture
of the entire system--

00:53:39.587 --> 00:53:40.920
AUDIENCE: I think I might have--

00:53:40.920 --> 00:53:43.810
AUDIENCE: --poured through
a relatively narrow pipe.

00:53:43.810 --> 00:53:47.560
And it's just very
sloppy and messy.

00:53:47.560 --> 00:53:50.830
It's-- also entails mixing
the manure and the water

00:53:50.830 --> 00:53:51.580
in a bucket.

00:53:51.580 --> 00:53:53.860
She had a little trowel
that she did it with.

00:53:53.860 --> 00:53:57.670
[INAUDIBLE] and I got nice and
dirty giving it a try as well.

00:53:57.670 --> 00:54:00.010
And yeah, it's just
a messy procedure.

00:54:00.010 --> 00:54:01.840
If we could come up
with some of device

00:54:01.840 --> 00:54:05.230
that would mix the
slurry in the bucket,

00:54:05.230 --> 00:54:08.320
as well as aid in pouring
it, not only would it

00:54:08.320 --> 00:54:10.630
make this a lot
easier to use and be

00:54:10.630 --> 00:54:13.690
able to make her-- or help
her get her chores done much

00:54:13.690 --> 00:54:17.500
faster, but it would
make biodigesters

00:54:17.500 --> 00:54:18.680
less messy in general.

00:54:18.680 --> 00:54:20.140
And I think that's--

00:54:20.140 --> 00:54:22.360
imagine that would be
an applicable market

00:54:22.360 --> 00:54:24.910
for biodigesters in the
states, biodigesters

00:54:24.910 --> 00:54:26.860
in other developing countries.

00:54:26.860 --> 00:54:29.530
It would be a very
transmutable project.

00:54:34.700 --> 00:54:37.892
AUDIENCE: So one
of the things we

00:54:37.892 --> 00:54:39.850
did a lot of thinking
about before we went down

00:54:39.850 --> 00:54:43.150
was doing a washing machine.

00:54:43.150 --> 00:54:46.400
So we had some extra
time [INAUDIBLE]

00:54:46.400 --> 00:54:47.860
we were waiting
for the biodigester

00:54:47.860 --> 00:54:51.320
to see if our levels
of gas was changing.

00:54:51.320 --> 00:54:53.770
So we talked to
several of the women

00:54:53.770 --> 00:54:57.520
and got them to show us how
they're washing clothes.

00:54:57.520 --> 00:55:02.680
In this case, they're doing it
in a lavandera, big concrete

00:55:02.680 --> 00:55:04.840
wash basin thing by our house.

00:55:04.840 --> 00:55:07.120
But most of the women were
actually washing them down

00:55:07.120 --> 00:55:08.950
by the river-- do
we have pictures--

00:55:14.855 --> 00:55:15.505
on stones.

00:55:15.505 --> 00:55:16.380
And you can't see it.

00:55:16.380 --> 00:55:21.060
There was a well off the
side of that picture.

00:55:21.060 --> 00:55:24.310
And we asked them a lot about--

00:55:24.310 --> 00:55:27.220
so washing the clothes
by hand involved

00:55:27.220 --> 00:55:31.450
a lot of vigorous scrubbing,
and also used a lot of water.

00:55:31.450 --> 00:55:34.720
In this case, we were using
about that entire 2-gallon

00:55:34.720 --> 00:55:37.180
bucket to wash one shirt.

00:55:37.180 --> 00:55:39.400
She said that she used--

00:55:39.400 --> 00:55:42.610
I believe it was about
30 gallons of water a day

00:55:42.610 --> 00:55:45.550
for doing the washing,
and she did it every day.

00:55:45.550 --> 00:55:48.730
She was only doing the
washing for three people,

00:55:48.730 --> 00:55:53.620
because most of her kids
were grown up and not around

00:55:53.620 --> 00:55:57.460
anymore-- so imagine that a
lot of families that are larger

00:55:57.460 --> 00:55:59.240
would use even more water.

00:55:59.240 --> 00:56:02.560
So we started asking
about what they

00:56:02.560 --> 00:56:05.710
think about the task of
washing, and whether they would

00:56:05.710 --> 00:56:09.040
like to use a machine or not.

00:56:09.040 --> 00:56:10.600
It turns out that
there's actually

00:56:10.600 --> 00:56:12.910
very mixed feelings about
the task of washing clothes.

00:56:12.910 --> 00:56:14.850
Some women really don't like it.

00:56:14.850 --> 00:56:17.413
Some enjoy it more
than other tasks,

00:56:17.413 --> 00:56:18.580
and so would rather do that.

00:56:18.580 --> 00:56:24.860
Some get bored and say
that it's something to do,

00:56:24.860 --> 00:56:28.450
and so they enjoy
it for that reason.

00:56:28.450 --> 00:56:34.530
And we asked them if they would
use a bicycle powered machine.

00:56:34.530 --> 00:56:36.567
In general, they were--

00:56:36.567 --> 00:56:38.150
we think they didn't
really understand

00:56:38.150 --> 00:56:42.000
the concept of powering
a machine off a bicycle.

00:56:42.000 --> 00:56:43.100
It was a very hilly area.

00:56:43.100 --> 00:56:45.020
We didn't see a lot of
bicycles, especially

00:56:45.020 --> 00:56:46.565
off the main roads
that were paved.

00:56:49.630 --> 00:56:55.420
And might have been better if
we had had pictures of that

00:56:55.420 --> 00:56:58.750
to show them the idea across,
but in general, the response

00:56:58.750 --> 00:57:04.730
was they didn't know if it
worked, and if it worked,

00:57:04.730 --> 00:57:08.800
if they liked it, they would use
it, but they couldn't say now.

00:57:08.800 --> 00:57:11.740
Some specific things about
[INAUDIBLE] is that they have

00:57:11.740 --> 00:57:13.810
a water--

00:57:13.810 --> 00:57:16.360
potable water system, and
they pay money for it.

00:57:16.360 --> 00:57:18.527
So there was a machine
up by the house.

00:57:18.527 --> 00:57:20.360
They'll do their washing
still by the river.

00:57:20.360 --> 00:57:22.672
So if there was a
machine up by the house--

00:57:22.672 --> 00:57:24.130
houses that they
would have to use,

00:57:24.130 --> 00:57:25.900
it would have to
use almost no water,

00:57:25.900 --> 00:57:29.840
because they don't want
to pay for the water.

00:57:29.840 --> 00:57:33.190
The well that was by
the river, which is--

00:57:33.190 --> 00:57:35.050
which we first thought
might be a good place

00:57:35.050 --> 00:57:38.410
to put a machine, was actually
built by a few families.

00:57:38.410 --> 00:57:40.897
And they own it,
and other people

00:57:40.897 --> 00:57:41.980
aren't supposed to use it.

00:57:41.980 --> 00:57:43.760
And they own the
land that it's there,

00:57:43.760 --> 00:57:46.810
so that would cause issues about
who could use the machine, who

00:57:46.810 --> 00:57:49.360
it was for.

00:57:49.360 --> 00:57:51.850
We considered a portable thing
that would be brought down

00:57:51.850 --> 00:57:54.370
to the river, but there's
already a lot to carry down,

00:57:54.370 --> 00:57:59.270
and it would have to be very
lightweight, and not bulky.

00:57:59.270 --> 00:58:03.590
And then, in general,
there isn't things

00:58:03.590 --> 00:58:06.140
that the women aren't
doing because they're

00:58:06.140 --> 00:58:07.010
washing clothes.

00:58:07.010 --> 00:58:10.580
It's not really taking time
that they would be spending

00:58:10.580 --> 00:58:11.940
making money or anything.

00:58:11.940 --> 00:58:15.410
And especially with the finances
in most households controlled

00:58:15.410 --> 00:58:18.920
by the men, there
isn't much motivation

00:58:18.920 --> 00:58:20.510
for them to spend
money on a machine

00:58:20.510 --> 00:58:22.302
to make the task of
washing clothes easier.

00:58:24.860 --> 00:58:28.130
In [INAUDIBLE],, we found that
there were a lot of obstacles,

00:58:28.130 --> 00:58:29.930
and we didn't think
that it could really be

00:58:29.930 --> 00:58:31.603
implemented successfully there.

00:58:31.603 --> 00:58:33.020
So we moved on to
[? El Jocote, ?]

00:58:33.020 --> 00:58:35.780
which was where we originally
imagined the project, and there

00:58:35.780 --> 00:58:37.115
was a better situation.

00:58:37.115 --> 00:58:38.990
They had one place by
the river that everyone

00:58:38.990 --> 00:58:42.650
washed for the dry season.

00:58:42.650 --> 00:58:44.900
There were already a
couple lavanderas there

00:58:44.900 --> 00:58:45.837
that people would--

00:58:45.837 --> 00:58:46.670
the women would use.

00:58:49.310 --> 00:58:52.070
And there's often
multiple women there.

00:58:52.070 --> 00:58:56.972
But again, it's only the dry
season that everyone's there.

00:58:56.972 --> 00:58:59.430
Everyone spreads out along the
river during the wet season.

00:58:59.430 --> 00:59:01.263
They're getting water
pumped to their houses

00:59:01.263 --> 00:59:05.010
soon, so people are unsure about
whether they're going to be

00:59:05.010 --> 00:59:06.670
washing up by their houses--

00:59:06.670 --> 00:59:08.878
especially if they have to
pay money for it-- or down

00:59:08.878 --> 00:59:10.750
by the river.

00:59:10.750 --> 00:59:12.750
There's a main path that
goes right by the place

00:59:12.750 --> 00:59:16.410
where they wash their clothes,
and animals-- lots of animals

00:59:16.410 --> 00:59:18.123
can buy to drink
and stuff, so it

00:59:18.123 --> 00:59:20.040
would need to be very
secure against the theft

00:59:20.040 --> 00:59:21.360
and the animals.

00:59:21.360 --> 00:59:23.940
We also heard that sometimes
the women will fight over

00:59:23.940 --> 00:59:26.880
the best spots to wash
their clothes, which--

00:59:26.880 --> 00:59:30.990
we would be afraid that a
machine that made it easier

00:59:30.990 --> 00:59:35.310
might exacerbate that, unless
it worked so quickly that no one

00:59:35.310 --> 00:59:38.910
minded the waiting time.

00:59:38.910 --> 00:59:40.650
And then we had
the same concerns

00:59:40.650 --> 00:59:45.270
about the possibility--
motivation

00:59:45.270 --> 00:59:48.840
for fronting money
for a machine.

00:59:48.840 --> 00:59:51.220
Water conservation was talked
about in both these cases,

00:59:51.220 --> 00:59:53.070
but we were there
in the dry season,

00:59:53.070 --> 00:59:55.500
and it didn't really
seem like they

00:59:55.500 --> 00:59:58.870
were concerned about actually
running out of water.

00:59:58.870 --> 01:00:02.820
So we're not sure that would
be a good motivation either.

01:00:02.820 --> 01:00:05.880
So even though we did a lot of
thinking about this beforehand,

01:00:05.880 --> 01:00:08.790
and the situation would
be slightly better

01:00:08.790 --> 01:00:11.250
in [? El Jocote, ?] we
don't think that it's really

01:00:11.250 --> 01:00:13.390
an appropriate
project to work on,

01:00:13.390 --> 01:00:19.220
especially compared to
other projects we saw.

01:00:19.220 --> 01:00:21.440
It seems to be more of
a solution in search

01:00:21.440 --> 01:00:25.500
of a problem than something
that needed to be solved.

01:00:25.500 --> 01:00:28.850
And if somebody really wanted
to work on a washing machine,

01:00:28.850 --> 01:00:31.220
the best way to do
it would be to--

01:00:31.220 --> 01:00:33.860
it could maybe be done
as a demonstration thing

01:00:33.860 --> 01:00:37.850
that AsoFenix would have to give
people a better idea of what

01:00:37.850 --> 01:00:40.280
we're talking about when
we say washing machines,

01:00:40.280 --> 01:00:43.310
and bicycle powered,
and stuff, but it

01:00:43.310 --> 01:00:45.350
would need to work
really well in order

01:00:45.350 --> 01:00:50.240
to convince them that it was
something worth switching to.

01:00:50.240 --> 01:00:52.450
So overall, we feel that a
lot of the other projects

01:00:52.450 --> 01:00:54.200
are far more worthwhile,
and this probably

01:00:54.200 --> 01:00:56.570
isn't something [INAUDIBLE]

01:00:56.570 --> 01:01:01.640
AUDIENCE: So our last project
was a millet thresher, which--

01:01:01.640 --> 01:01:06.920
this is millet here on the
head, and this is the raw grain

01:01:06.920 --> 01:01:08.790
after the processing.

01:01:08.790 --> 01:01:14.120
So in the town of
[? El Jocote, ?] they--

01:01:14.120 --> 01:01:17.750
most of the families
grow millet.

01:01:17.750 --> 01:01:21.780
And the processing of this
plant takes a long time.

01:01:21.780 --> 01:01:25.020
It takes a total--

01:01:25.020 --> 01:01:30.710
so to harvest the fields, it
takes between four to six days

01:01:30.710 --> 01:01:32.150
of one man working.

01:01:32.150 --> 01:01:33.650
So they normally
have two men do it.

01:01:33.650 --> 01:01:38.100
It takes between two and
three days to do that.

01:01:38.100 --> 01:01:42.830
Then they wait 8 to 15
days for the heads to dry--

01:01:42.830 --> 01:01:44.090
or for the stalks to dry.

01:01:44.090 --> 01:01:50.150
And then they cut the heads
off of the stalks, which takes

01:01:50.150 --> 01:01:53.510
another two to four man days.

01:01:53.510 --> 01:01:56.420
And then the long,
time-consuming part

01:01:56.420 --> 01:01:59.000
is knocking the
little pieces of grain

01:01:59.000 --> 01:02:03.290
off of the head of the stock.

01:02:03.290 --> 01:02:08.240
And the way they do this
is they have this device--

01:02:08.240 --> 01:02:12.770
or platform here, where
they stand on that plank,

01:02:12.770 --> 01:02:16.970
and then take the stick
and hit the heads.

01:02:16.970 --> 01:02:21.140
And then the grain falls out,
because that's just pieces--

01:02:21.140 --> 01:02:24.410
individual sticks going across
the bottom of the platform,

01:02:24.410 --> 01:02:26.840
so the grains just fall
through onto the tarp.

01:02:26.840 --> 01:02:29.990
Normally they'd have a bigger
tarp at the bottom of this,

01:02:29.990 --> 01:02:32.915
but they just saved the
one bag, and their harvest

01:02:32.915 --> 01:02:35.360
had ended before we got there.

01:02:35.360 --> 01:02:37.940
But Jaime, the
director of AsoFenix,

01:02:37.940 --> 01:02:39.860
requested that they
save a bag to show us,

01:02:39.860 --> 01:02:41.600
so this is just one bag.

01:02:41.600 --> 01:02:43.820
They would normally
have a bigger tarp.

01:02:43.820 --> 01:02:46.640
And then there's a guy
below who's sweeping,

01:02:46.640 --> 01:02:51.350
and that's because parts of
the head stock-- not the grain,

01:02:51.350 --> 01:02:52.850
but the head stock--
also fall down.

01:02:52.850 --> 01:02:56.330
And he's sweeping
those black bits

01:02:56.330 --> 01:03:00.380
out of the pile of pure grain.

01:03:00.380 --> 01:03:01.940
So normally in their
process, they'd

01:03:01.940 --> 01:03:03.920
have two men on the
plank-- one man sweeping,

01:03:03.920 --> 01:03:08.180
and then another man loading
more raw material up on top.

01:03:08.180 --> 01:03:10.280
So it's a four-man
process, and it

01:03:10.280 --> 01:03:17.330
takes anywhere between 4
and 10 days for each family

01:03:17.330 --> 01:03:18.938
to process their
harvest, depending

01:03:18.938 --> 01:03:20.480
on their size of
the field, depending

01:03:20.480 --> 01:03:23.130
on the yield for that year.

01:03:23.130 --> 01:03:26.540
So it's a lot of time, because
that's four men working

01:03:26.540 --> 01:03:27.750
for 4 to 10 days.

01:03:27.750 --> 01:03:35.920
So that's upwards of 40 man
days to complete this task.

01:03:35.920 --> 01:03:40.130
The men don't like doing this
work, so there's a very easy--

01:03:40.130 --> 01:03:42.020
if the machine works,
they will use it.

01:03:46.160 --> 01:03:47.910
It's as simple as that.

01:03:47.910 --> 01:03:51.110
Then, when you hit the
heads, dust is emitted,

01:03:51.110 --> 01:03:53.690
and that stings your skin,
makes it really itchy.

01:03:53.690 --> 01:03:56.090
And it's also not
very good to breathe.

01:03:56.090 --> 01:03:59.600
And the man sweeping below
is just engulfed in this dust

01:03:59.600 --> 01:04:02.690
during the entire process.

01:04:02.690 --> 01:04:05.400
They don't necessarily
switch jobs that often,

01:04:05.400 --> 01:04:08.443
so it could be one
man just sweeping.

01:04:08.443 --> 01:04:09.860
So what they do
with this millet--

01:04:09.860 --> 01:04:10.820
they cook with it.

01:04:10.820 --> 01:04:15.950
They feed the leftover
heads and the mixed grain

01:04:15.950 --> 01:04:21.170
that the guy sweeping out
the bits of black heads

01:04:21.170 --> 01:04:22.008
to livestock.

01:04:22.008 --> 01:04:23.800
Or they can also separate
it, because the--

01:04:23.800 --> 01:04:27.260
in water, the grains sink,
and the parts of the stock

01:04:27.260 --> 01:04:30.740
stay floating.

01:04:30.740 --> 01:04:33.620
So let's see.

01:04:33.620 --> 01:04:35.600
For this project, if--

01:04:35.600 --> 01:04:37.340
it would be great to
have a machine that

01:04:37.340 --> 01:04:40.070
gets like 80% of the
seeds, because this hitting

01:04:40.070 --> 01:04:42.740
process doesn't get--

01:04:42.740 --> 01:04:46.100
doesn't even get close to 100%
of the seeds out of this--

01:04:46.100 --> 01:04:46.680
the head.

01:04:46.680 --> 01:04:49.160
So if you were to
get 80% of the seeds,

01:04:49.160 --> 01:04:52.910
that would be acceptable
for a machine,

01:04:52.910 --> 01:04:55.190
especially given that--

01:04:55.190 --> 01:04:58.040
their efficiency right now.

01:04:58.040 --> 01:05:00.020
And then also, if you
had a way to control

01:05:00.020 --> 01:05:05.090
the dust from expanding
off of the machine,

01:05:05.090 --> 01:05:11.260
or if you could
keep it sequestered,

01:05:11.260 --> 01:05:14.230
that would also increase
the health of the machine.

01:05:20.280 --> 01:05:22.913
So that's it.

01:05:22.913 --> 01:05:24.080
PROFESSOR: Thanks, you guys.

01:05:24.080 --> 01:05:25.382
[APPLAUSE]

01:05:26.250 --> 01:05:27.380
Do you have a question?

01:05:27.380 --> 01:05:30.376
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
I have a question.

01:05:30.376 --> 01:05:34.150
Is there a reason why
it's so high above?

01:05:34.150 --> 01:05:36.195
Is it just literally
sticks, or is there

01:05:36.195 --> 01:05:38.430
some sort of [INAUDIBLE]

01:05:38.430 --> 01:05:42.390
AUDIENCE: So it's literally
just sticks all the way around.

01:05:42.390 --> 01:05:44.440
You can see the walls
better from here,

01:05:44.440 --> 01:05:48.180
but the bottom is the same.

01:05:48.180 --> 01:05:50.130
We thought the reason
that it's so high

01:05:50.130 --> 01:05:53.100
is so that you can have a
man down there sweeping,

01:05:53.100 --> 01:05:54.930
and if-- he's not hunched over.

01:05:58.900 --> 01:06:00.070
I don't know.

01:06:00.070 --> 01:06:02.292
AUDIENCE: As it
falls down to the--

01:06:02.292 --> 01:06:04.750
the dust that's with
it-- they don't really

01:06:04.750 --> 01:06:06.650
want that to get in
the rest of the grain,

01:06:06.650 --> 01:06:10.060
so when it's falling down,
the wind will blow it away,

01:06:10.060 --> 01:06:13.588
so that gives more time for
that to happen too probably.

01:06:13.588 --> 01:06:23.562
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:06:23.562 --> 01:06:25.270
AUDIENCE: There were
two in the community

01:06:25.270 --> 01:06:27.970
that-- of [INAUDIBLE]
that we went to.

01:06:27.970 --> 01:06:30.010
We only saw the one.

01:06:30.010 --> 01:06:34.060
They have about 10 AsoFenix
that they've, I guess,

01:06:34.060 --> 01:06:35.560
installed already,
and they're going

01:06:35.560 --> 01:06:37.480
to continue installing more.

01:06:37.480 --> 01:06:41.290
Only two of those 10
are operating right now,

01:06:41.290 --> 01:06:43.450
successfully running.

01:06:43.450 --> 01:06:47.410
And that was including the
one that we were working with

01:06:47.410 --> 01:06:49.052
[INAUDIBLE], which--

01:06:49.052 --> 01:06:51.010
we're not really sure
how much she's feeding it

01:06:51.010 --> 01:06:53.120
and how much she's
actually using it.

01:06:53.120 --> 01:06:54.470
So I don't know.

01:06:54.470 --> 01:06:57.640
It's hard to say.

01:06:57.640 --> 01:06:59.500
A big thing-- we're
not sure whether

01:06:59.500 --> 01:07:01.045
it's because of
the mixing process,

01:07:01.045 --> 01:07:03.700
that it just is unappealing to--

01:07:03.700 --> 01:07:06.430
what is the logjam
that's creating

01:07:06.430 --> 01:07:10.850
this gap between getting
the gas, using it or not?

01:07:10.850 --> 01:07:15.370
So if it's that mixing, and we
can take the human element--

01:07:15.370 --> 01:07:17.440
human handling out
of that process

01:07:17.440 --> 01:07:19.823
if they would use it
every day, then that

01:07:19.823 --> 01:07:20.990
would make a big difference.

01:07:20.990 --> 01:07:26.500
But if there's another problem,
we'd have to address it more.

